EXT. DESERT WELL - DUSK

A dry well, a rope, a bucket. Sage and rock. The sun is a red coin.

AMOS, 60s, a grizzled prospector, hauls the bucket up hand over hand. Empty. He tosses it aside. Beside him, HESTER, 30s, in a long gray coat, holds a rifle loosely.

A STRANGER, 40s, stands thirty yards off on a ridge, a black silhouette against the sky.

HESTER
He's been there an hour.

AMOS
He's patient.

HESTER
Or dumb.

AMOS
Maybe both.

The Stranger lifts a hand. He starts down the slope, slow, each step careful.

HESTER
Want me to?

AMOS
Not yet.

He reaches the well. Close up, he's older than he seemed, with a gray beard and a limp. He tips his hat.

STRANGER
Afternoon.

AMOS
Evening.

STRANGER
Is there water?

AMOS
Not for a week.

The Stranger nods. He sits on a rock, puts down his pack.

STRANGER
I'm Calloway. Samuel Calloway.

HESTER
(muttering)
Great.

CALLOWAY
You know me.

HESTER
We know the name.

Amos stiffens. His hand goes to his belt. Hester's thumb finds the hammer.

CALLOWAY
I'm not here for trouble.

AMOS
Then why?

CALLOWAY
Because the man who shot your son is dead.

A beat. The wind drops. Hester's rifle sags. Amos stares at the Stranger for a long moment, his eyes wet in the red light.

AMOS
Who did it?

CALLOWAY
I did.

Silence. Amos turns, picks up the bucket, and walks back to the well. He hangs it on its hook. His hand lingers on the rope.

HESTER
Amos.

AMOS
(without turning)
Give him your canteen.

Hester doesn't move. Then, slowly, she unslings it and tosses it to the man on the rock.
